1. Create a new directory called mysite in your home folder, place an index.html file inside it, and set permissions so only your user can read and access the site files.
=>mysite:mkdir -p /root/mysite

cat > /root/mysite/index.html << 'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>My Private Site</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to My Private Site</h1>
    <p>This site is restricted to the owner only.</p>
</body>
</html>

chown -R root:www-data /root/mysite
chmod 750 /root/mysite          # owner rwx, group (www-data) r-x, others ---
chmod 640 /root/mysite/index.html # owner rw, group r, others ---
chmod 711 /root                 # allow path traversal (x) without listing
2. Configure your Apache or Nginx server to serve the mysite directory as a website, then test if you can access it from your browser while logged in as your user.
=>cat > /etc/apache2/conf-available/mysite.conf << 'EOF'
Alias /mysite /root/mysite

<Directory /root/mysite>
    Options Indexes FollowSymLinks
    AllowOverride None
    Require all granted
</Directory>

a2enconf mysite
service apache2 reload

3. Change the permissions of the mysite directory so that another user on the system cannot access the site files directly. Try to access the files as the other user and note the result.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use chmod and su or a different terminal window for the second user.</em>
=>useradd -m -s /bin/bash otheruser
su - otheruser -c 'ls -ld /root/mysite; ls /root/mysite; cat /root/mysite/index.html'
results:    drwxr-x--- 2 root www-data 4096 ... /root/mysite
ls: cannot open directory '/root/mysite': Permission denied
cat: /root/mysite/index.html: Permission denied

